CAT = 'hknLaw'; SEG = 'LAW'
Q = [
('Which Hong Kong ordinance governs the registration of nurses?',
 'Nurses Registration Ordinance', ['Medical Registration Ordinance', 'Mental Health Ordinance', 'Hospital Authority Ordinance'],
 'The Nurses Registration Ordinance sets up the Nursing Council and the registers of nurses and enrolled nurses. The other ordinances deal with different matters.'),
("Which is one of the four domains of the Nursing Council's Code of Ethics and Professional Conduct?",
 'Nurses and Society', ['Nurses and Employers', 'Nurses and Government', 'Nurses and Insurers'],
 "The Code's four domains are Nurses and People, Nurses and Practice, Nurses and the Profession, and Nurses and Society."),
("A nurse tells a neighbour about a client's diagnosis. Which ethical standard is breached?",
 'Keeping personal data confidential', ['Fair allocation of resources', 'Commitment to professional growth', 'Collaboration with co-workers'],
 'The Code requires nurses to keep personal information obtained in a professional capacity in lawful confidence.'),
('Which set of features makes consent to treatment valid?',
 'Competent, informed and voluntary', ['Signed by a relative or friend', 'Given verbally by any staff member', 'Obtained once the procedure begins'],
 'Valid consent comes from a person with capacity, who has understood adequate information and agrees freely.'),
('Touching a competent client without consent amounts to which tort?',
 'Battery', ['Defamation', 'Fraud', 'False imprisonment'],
 'Battery is unlawful physical contact. Assault is the threat of such contact.'),
('What must a claimant show to prove negligence against a nurse?',
 'A breach of duty that caused harm', ['Any poor clinical outcome', 'An intention to injure the client', 'A written complaint from a relative'],
 'Negligence needs a duty of care, a breach of the standard expected, and harm caused by that breach.'),
("An employer is liable for an employee's negligent acts done in the course of work. What is this principle called?",
 'Vicarious liability', ['Strict liability', 'Contributory negligence', 'Absolute privilege'],
 "Vicarious liability makes the employer answerable for employees' wrongs at work. The nurse remains personally accountable as well."),
('How many data protection principles are set out in the Personal Data (Privacy) Ordinance?',
 'Six', ['Three', 'Nine', 'Twelve'],
 'The Ordinance sets out six data protection principles covering collection, accuracy and retention, use, security, openness and access.'),
('A supplier offers a nurse a personal gift hoping to influence a purchasing decision. What is the correct action?',
 'Decline it and report it', ['Accept it if the value is small', 'Accept it and share it with the ward', 'Accept it after the order is placed'],
 'Accepting an advantage to influence official or employment decisions can offend the Bribery Ordinance. The nurse declines and reports under policy.'),
('A competent client insists on leaving hospital against advice. What should the nurse do?',
 'Tell the doctor and explain the risks', ['Hold the client until a relative arrives', 'Let the client go without telling anyone', "Keep the client's belongings"],
 'A competent adult may leave. The nurse informs the doctor, explains the risks, and documents the discussion.'),
('Which tort arises if a nurse restrains a competent client without lawful authority?',
 'False imprisonment', ['Defamation of character', 'Fraud by misrepresentation', 'Negligence causing harm'],
 "False imprisonment is unlawful restriction of a person's freedom of movement. Restraint needs legal authority and clear clinical justification."),
('A competent adult refuses a life-sustaining treatment after a full explanation. Which principle guides the nurse?',
 'Respect for autonomy', ['Beneficence (doing good)', 'Distributive justice', 'Veracity (truthfulness)'],
 "Autonomy is the right to make informed decisions about one's own care, including refusal, even when others disagree."),
]
